{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"diagnosis\":\"Use f itself as the test function. Its self-inner-product is nonnegative and integrable, so the zero integral implies it vanishes almost everywhere; continuity upgrades this to pointwise vanishing, and definiteness of the inner product gives f = 0.\",\"proof_body\":\"by\\n  have hf' := hg f hf\\n  rw [MeasureTheory.integral_eq_zero_iff_of_nonneg] at hf'\\n  · rw [Continuous.ae_eq_iff_eq] at hf'\\n    · funext x\\n      have hf'' := congrFun hf' x\\n      simpa using hf''\\n    · have hfc : Continuous f := hf.smooth.continuous\\n      fun_prop\\n    · fun_prop\\n  · intro x\\n    simp only [Pi.zero_apply]\\n    apply real_inner_self_nonneg'\\n  · apply IsTestFunction.integrable\\n    exact IsTestFunction.inner hf hf\"}"}}
